**Chapter 0: The Prince of Static Streets – Part 1**

Crimson walked like the rain couldn’t touch him.

It tried—oh, it tried, drizzling down in streaks of neon blue and grime-filtered amber, clinging to rusted signs and digital prayers half-glitched on the undercity walls—but it never quite reached him. His coat flared just enough to look accidental. His stride had that infuriating tilt of someone who knew the weight of the world and had decided it wasn’t *his* to carry—just to dance on.

He passed a mural of himself without slowing down.

It was faded now—paint chipping, tags scrawled over the signature. *“CRIMSON = CONTROLLED OPPOSITION”* someone had scrawled over the left cheek. Someone else had drawn devil horns. Someone else had tried to scrub it clean.

He smirked. “Still better than my Academy portrait.”

“Hey, look who’s slumming it!”

A voice called from a noodle stall wedged between two ventilation ducts. The vendor, Goma, was ex-militia and current soup wizard, her arms covered in servo tattoos that moved when she stirred. She grinned at him like an old aunt who didn’t care that he’d once made national headlines for ‘accidentally’ blowing up an orbital drone station.

“Come to grace the filth with that pretty jawline?”

Crimson leaned on the stall’s edge. “The jawline graces where it wills. Got anything that won’t kill me today?”

“Nope.”

“Perfect.”

Goma handed him a bowl of steaming synth-pork and something that still twitched.

He sat on a crate, chopsticks whirring in one hand, scanning the street. The undercity pulsed like a heart too stubborn to quit. Street performers danced in LED ash. Kids ran between drone legs, laughing like the war had never touched them. Merchants haggled under faulty lamplight while micro-preachers screamed about digital salvation.

This was *home*. Not by comfort—but by recognition.

Halfway through the bowl, a kid approached him. Twelve? Thirteen? Eyes too old, boots too big.

“You really him?” the kid asked. “The Crimson?”

Crimson wiped his mouth with a fingerless glove. “Depends who’s asking.”

The kid shrugged. “Got a bet riding on it. Five chips says you’re just another cosplayer with a good jaw.”

Crimson winked. “Tell you what—ask me something only the real Crimson would know.”

The kid thought. “What’s the name of the mech you took down in the Hallow Spire with nothing but a magnetic spoon and a bad attitude?”

Crimson raised an eyebrow. “Vox-Tier Theta Prime.”

The kid’s grin cracked wide. “That’s what I said! Pay up, Jun!”

Somewhere behind a tarp stall, a groan.

“See?” Crimson stood, dropping the empty bowl in the cleaner drone’s tray. “Public service. Still got it.”

He saluted Goma, let the crowd swallow him.

And deeper into the city he walked.

Toward the throne. Toward the chains. Toward the end of something too golden to last.

